1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,261
Real-world manipulation is compositional: one instruction, many subtasks.

2
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:11,490
World-action models predict what happens next — but not which intermediate state to pursue.

3
00:00:12,590 --> 00:00:16,160
Meet ViGAR: Visual Goal-conditioned Action Reasoning.

4
00:00:17,261 --> 00:00:20,617
A subgoal planner imagines the next subgoal as an image.

5
00:00:20,767 --> 00:00:24,928
A world-action policy then generates the video and actions that reach it.

6
00:00:25,078 --> 00:00:30,235
Both share one pretrained world model, and the loop repeats at every control step.

7
00:00:31,335 --> 00:00:36,575
On a real AgiBot A2, ViGAR completes five long-horizon tasks,

8
00:00:36,725 --> 00:00:44,137
with an average task progress of 0.69, ahead of π0.5 and a Cosmos3 policy trained on the same data.

9
00:00:45,237 --> 00:00:50,526
Hand the planner a global goal image, and the same scene and instruction turn into a different plan —

10
00:00:50,676 --> 00:00:53,489
unseen compositions, zero parameter updates.

11
00:00:56,237 --> 00:01:03,929
On RoboTwin, ViGAR reaches 67% under randomized scenes, 25 points above the best prior world-action model.

12
00:01:05,029 --> 00:01:08,008
ViGAR. Plan in pixels, act with a world model.

